        <!-- Section 6: Key Takeaways -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Key Takeaways & Outlook</h2>

            <div class="key-finding warning">
                <strong>1. Unprecedented Growth:</strong> The satellite population has grown by over {starlink_count/total_satellites*100:.0f}% in just a few years,
                primarily due to Starlink. This rate of expansion exceeds all previous decades combined.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>2. LEO Congestion:</strong> {leo_share:.1f}% of all active satellites are in LEO, creating significant challenges for
                orbital debris mitigation, spectrum coordination, and collision avoidance.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding accent">
                <strong>3. Single-Operator Dominance:</strong> SpaceX controls {starlink_count/total_satellites*100:.1f}% of the active satellite population.
                This concentration raises policy questions about market competition, spectrum access, and space sustainability.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>4. Constellation Shell Architecture:</strong> Starlink's multi-shell inclination strategy (53°, 70°, 97.6°)
                demonstrates how mega-constellations achieve global coverage while optimizing launch efficiency.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding warning">
                <strong>5. Policy Implications:</strong> The rapid growth of LEO constellations necessitates updated frameworks for:
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                    <li>Orbital debris mitigation and deorbit requirements</li>
                    <li>Spectrum allocation and interference management</li>
                    <li>Licensing coordination among competing constellations</li>
                    <li>Space traffic management and collision avoidance</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Summary Statistics</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><td>Total Active Satellites</td><td class="number"><strong>{total_satellites:,}</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>LEO Share</td><td class="number"><strong>{leo_share:.1f}%</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Starlink Satellites</td><td class="number"><strong>{starlink_count:,} ({starlink_count/total_satellites*100:.1f}%)</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Median Inclination</td><td class="number"><strong>{median_inclination:.1f}°</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Post-2020 Launches</td><td class="number"><strong>{post_2020_count:,} ({post_2020_count/total_satellites*100:.1f}%)</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Top 5 Operators' Share</td><td class="number"><strong>{constellation_counts.head(5).sum()/total_satellites*100:.1f}%</strong></td></tr>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="footer">
        <p>Report generated from CelesTrak/NORAD TLE data | {datetime.now().strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")}</p>
        <p>Analysis by Claude Code | Active Satellites in Earth Orbit — 2026 Catalog Analysis</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
'''

